.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt STAT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm stat
.Nd display file status
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm stat
.Op Fl FLnq
.Oo
.Fl f Ar format |
.Fl l |
.Fl r |
.Fl s |
.Fl x
.Oc
.Op Fl t Ar timefmt
.Op Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility displays information about the file pointed to by
.Ar file .
Read, write, or execute permissions of the named file are
not required, but all directories listed in the pathname
leading to the file must be searchable.
If no argument is given,
.Nm
displays information about the file descriptor for
standard input.
.Pp
.Nm
follows the BSD interface, as on
.Ox
and macOS.
Where BSD and GNU assign different meanings to the same
flag, vibeutils follows BSD; GNU's long spellings are kept
alongside, since a long option is not a collision.
Two consequences are easy to misread:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Fl f
is
.Em not
an alias of
.Fl Fl file-system ,
and
.Fl t
is
.Em not
an alias of
.Fl Fl terse .
BSD defines the short spellings; GNU defines the long ones.
.It
Two format-directive languages coexist, selected by the flag
that introduced the string.
.Fl c ,
.Fl Fl format
and
.Fl Fl printf
parse GNU directives;
.Fl f
parses the BSD directives described below.
.El
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl F
As in
.Xr ls 1 ,
display a slash
.Pq Ql /
immediately after each pathname that is a directory, an
asterisk
.Pq Ql *
after each that is executable, an at sign
.Pq Ql @
after each symbolic link, an equal sign
.Pq Ql =
after each socket, and a vertical bar
.Pq Ql |
after each that is a FIFO.
The use of
.Fl F
implies
.Fl l .
.It Fl L , Fl Fl dereference
Use
.Xr stat 2
instead of
.Xr lstat 2 .
The information reported refers to the target of
.Ar file
if it is a symbolic link.
If the link is broken or the target does not exist, fall
back on
.Xr lstat 2
and report information about the link.
.It Fl f Ar format
Display information using the specified format.
See
.Sx FORMATS .
.It Fl l
Display output in
.Ic ls -lT
format.
.It Fl n
Do not force a newline to appear at the end of each piece
of output.
.It Fl q
Suppress failure messages if calls to
.Xr stat 2
or
.Xr lstat 2
fail.
.It Fl r
Display raw information: for all fields in the stat
structure, the raw numerical value, such as times in
seconds since the Epoch.
.It Fl s
Display information in shell output format, suitable for
initializing variables.
.It Fl t Ar timefmt
Display timestamps using the specified format, passed
directly to
.Xr strftime 3 .
.It Fl x
Display information in a more verbose way, as known from
some Linux distributions.
.It Fl c Ar FORMAT , Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Ar FORMAT
Use the specified GNU-style
.Ar FORMAT ,
outputting a newline after each use of it.
.It Fl Fl printf Ns = Ns Ar FORMAT
Like
.Fl Fl format
but interpret backslash escapes and do not output a
mandatory trailing newline.
.It Fl Fl file-system
Display file system status instead of file status
.Pq GNU behavior; see the note above .
.It Fl Fl terse
Print the information in GNU terse form.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Display a help message and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl Fl version
Output version information and exit.
.El
.Pp
.Fl f , Fl l , Fl r , Fl s
and
.Fl x
are mutually exclusive.
.Ss FORMATS
Format strings are similar to
.Xr printf 3
formats: they start with
.Ql % ,
are followed by a sequence of formatting characters, and
end in a character selecting the field of
.Vt struct stat
to format.
If the
.Ql %
is immediately followed by
.Cm n , t , %
or
.Cm @ ,
then a newline, a tab, a percent character, or the current
file number is printed.
Otherwise the string is examined for the following.
.Pp
Any of these optional flags:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm #
Select an alternate form for octal and hexadecimal output.
.It Cm +
Always print a sign for numeric output.
.It Cm -
Align string output to the left of the field.
.It Cm 0
Pad on the left with
.Ql 0
rather than spaces.
.It Cm space
Reserve a space at the front of non-negative signed output.
A
.Ql +
overrides a space if both are used.
.El
.Pp
Then the following fields:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar size
An optional decimal digit string giving the minimum field
width.
.It Ar prec
An optional precision, a
.Ql \&.
followed by a decimal digit string.
.It Ar fmt
An optional output format, one of
.Cm D , O , U , X , F
or
.Cm S
for signed decimal, octal, unsigned decimal, hexadecimal,
floating point, and string output respectively.
Floating point applies only to the
.Cm a , m
and
.Cm c
fields.
.Pp
The
.Cm S
specifier may be combined with:
.Bl -tag -width "amc" -compact
.It Cm amc
Display date in
.Xr strftime 3
format.
.It Cm dr
Display actual device name.
.It Cm f
Display the file flags as in
.Ic ls -lTdo .
.It Cm gu
Display group or user name.
.It Cm p
Display the mode as in
.Ic ls -lTd .
.It Cm N
Display the name of the file.
.It Cm T
Display the type of the file.
.It Cm Y
Insert
.Ql " -> "
into the output.
.El
.It Ar sub
An optional sub-field specifier, applying only to the
.Cm p , d , r
and
.Cm T
formats:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm H
High: the major number for devices, the user bits of a
string-form mode, the type bits of a numeric mode, or the
long form of
.Cm T .
.It Cm M
Middle: the group bits of a string-form mode, or the setuid,
setgid and sticky bits of a numeric mode.
.It Cm L
Low: the minor number for devices, the other bits of a
string-form mode, or the
.Ic ls -F
style character for
.Cm T .
.El
.It Ar datum
A required field specifier, one of:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm d
Device upon which the file resides.
.It Cm i
Inode number.
.It Cm p
File type and permissions.
.It Cm l
Number of hard links.
.It Cm u , g
User ID and group ID of the owner.
.It Cm r
Device number for character and block special files.
.It Cm a , m , c , B
Time of last access or modification, time the inode was
last changed, or the birth time of the inode.
.It Cm z
Size of the file in bytes.
.It Cm b
Number of blocks allocated.
.It Cm k
Optimal file system I/O block size.
.It Cm f
User-defined flags.
Always zero on Linux, which has no equivalent field.
.It Cm v
Inode generation number.
Always zero on Linux.
.It Cm N
The name of the file.
.It Cm R
The absolute pathname corresponding to the file.
.It Cm T
The file type, as in
.Ic ls -F
or, with the
.Cm H
sub-field, in a more descriptive form.
.It Cm Y
The target of a symbolic link.
.It Cm Z
Expands to
.Dq major,minor
for character or block special files, and to the size for
all others.
.El
.El
.Pp
Only the
.Ql %
and the datum are required.
Most datums default to
.Cm U ;
.Cm p
defaults to
.Cm O ;
.Cm a , m
and
.Cm c
default to
.Cm D ;
and
.Cm Y , T
and
.Cm N
default to
.Cm S .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std stat
.Sh EXAMPLES
With no options the default format is equivalent to:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
%d %i %Sp %l %Su %Sg %r %z "%Sa" "%Sm" "%Sc" "%SB" %k %b %#Xf %N
.Ed
.Pp
producing one line per file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ stat /tmp/bar
0 78852 -rw-r--r-- 1 root wheel 0 0 "Jul  8 10:26:03 2004" ...
.Ed
.Pp
Determine the permissions set on a file separately:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ stat -f "%Sp -> owner=%SHp group=%SMp other=%SLp" .
drwxr-xr-x -> owner=rwx group=r-x other=r-x
.Ed
.Pp
Display a modification time as an epoch, readable, and in a
sortable form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ stat -f %m /tmp/foo
1177697733
$ stat -f %Sm /tmp/foo
Apr 27 11:15:33 2007
$ stat -f %Sm -t %Y%m%d%H%M%S /tmp/foo
20070427111533
.Ed
.Pp
Initialize shell variables:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ eval $(stat -s .profile)
$ echo $st_size
1148
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ls 1 ,
.Xr test 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is not specified by POSIX.
The interface follows the BSD
.Nm
of
.Ox
and macOS.
The GNU interface is reachable through the long options
.Fl Fl format ,
.Fl Fl printf ,
.Fl Fl file-system
and
.Fl Fl terse .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
